"Vilkins v. United States, 598 U.S. 152, 143 S.Ct. 870, 215 L.Ed.2d 116 (2023). Plaintiffs brought suit against the United States under the Quiet Title Act regarding the scope of the government’s easement for use of a road on the plaintiffs’ land. The government moved to dismiss based on 28 U.S.C. § 2409a(g), which provides that such an action “shall be barred unless it is commenced within twelve years of the date upon which it ccrued.” In a six-to-three decision, the Court held that the 12-year time limit was not jurisdictional. The majority emphasized the clear-statement rule it had declared in earlier cases: that “this Court will treat a procedural requirement as jurisdictional only if Congress clearly states that it is.” (internal quotation marks omitted). It explained: “This principle of onstruction is not a burden courts impose on Congress. To the contrary, this principle seeks to avoid judicial interpretations that undermine Congress’ judgment. Loosely treating procedural requirements as jurisdictional risks undermining the very reason Congress enacted them.” Turning to the Quiet Title Act’s 12-year time limit, the Court noted that, in applying the clear-statement rule, “we have made plain that most time bars are nonjurisdictional.” It concluded that “[nJothing about § 2409a(g)’s text or context gives reason to depart from this beaten path.” The dissenting justices urged that the clear-statement rule did not apply ecause § 2409a(g) was “a condition on a waiver of sovereign immunity,” but the majority rejected that position as contrary to long-standing precedent. TOAC Mall Holdings LLC v. Transform Holdco LLC, 598 U.S. 288, 143 S.Ct. 927, 215 L.Ed.2d 262 (2023). In a unanimous decision, the Court addressed whether a provision of the federal Bankruptcy Code limiting the effect of certain appellate decisions was jurisdictional. Under 11 U.S.C. § 363, interested parties may appeal a Bankruptcy Court’s order authorizing a debtor or trustee to sell or lease certain assets. But § 363(m) restricts the extent to which the ppellate court’s reversal or modification of such an order will affect the validity of that previously authorized sale or lease. The Court concluded that § 363(m)’s restriction was not jurisdictional. It noted that “[t]he ‘Jurisdictional’ label is significant because it carries with it unique and sometimes severe consequences.” The failure of a “jurisdictional precondition” requires “immediate dismissal” and is “impervious to excuses like waiver or forfeiture.” And federal courts must raise nd enforce jurisdictional rules sua sponte. Accordingly, the Court stressed once again that ‘we only treat a provision as jurisdictional if Congress clearly states as much.” (quotation marks omitted): 507 This clear-statement rule implements “Congress” likely intent” regarding whether noncompliance with a precondition “governs a court’s adjudicatory capacity.” * * * We have reasoned that “Congress ordinarily enacts preconditions to facilitate the fair and orderly disposition of litigation and would not heedlessly give those same rules an unusual character that threatens to upend that orderly progress.” * * * he Court noted, however, that “Congress need not use magic words to convey its intent that a statutory precondition be treated as jurisdictional.” (quotation marks omitted). It is possible for traditional tools of statutory construction to “reveal a clear statement,” but “the statement must indeed be clear; it is insufficient that a jurisdictional reading is ‘plausible,’ or ven ‘better,’ than nonjurisdictional alternatives.” Applying these principles, the Court found that § 363(m)’s limits did not purport to restrict “a court’s adjudicatory capacity.” Section 363(m) was merely “a caveated constraint on the effect of a reversal or modification,” and the Court noted that the constraint itself would be inapplicable in certain circumstances, such as “where the sale or lease was made to a ad-faith purchaser or lessee, or if the sale or lease is stayed pending appeal.” No clear statement dictated that § 363(m) be read as a jurisdictional limitation. antos-Zacaria v. Garland, 598 U.S. 411, 143 8.Ct. 1103, 215 L.Ed.2d 375 (2023). Petitioner Santos-Zacaria was apprehended by U.S. immigration authorities after fleeing from her native Guatemala. She sought withholding of removal based on the likelihood of persecution in Guatemala. The Immigration Judge within the Department of Justice rejected her argument, and she petitioned for review in U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth ‘ircuit. The Fifth Circuit dismissed one of petitioner’s arguments for lack of jurisdiction on the basis that she had failed to exhaust administrative remedies under 8 U.S.C. § 1252(d)(1). One of the issues before the Supreme Court was whether that exhaustion requirement was jurisdictional. Justice Jackson’s majority opinion (on behalf of seven justices) concluded that § 1252(d)(1) “is not jurisdictional and is therefore “subject to waiver and forfeiture.” Noting the clear-statement rule described in the Court’s earlier cases, Justice Jackson wrote that if “multiple plausible interpretations exist—only one of which is jurisdictional—tt is difficult to take the case that the jurisdictional reading is clear.” An exhaustion requirement like § 1252(d)(1) “is a quintessential claim-processing rule” that ordinarily is not jurisdictional, and Justice Jackson observed that “we have yet to hold that any statutory exhaustion requirement is jurisdictional when applying the clear-statement rule.” Jurisdictional treatment of an exhaustion requirement could undo the benefits of exhaustion. That is, exhaustion promotes efficiency, including by encouraging parties to resolve their disputes without litigation. * * * But jurisdictional treatment can result in the opposite: If exhaustion is jurisdictional, litigants must slog through preliminary nonjudicial proceedings even when, for example, no party demands it or a court finds it would be "